section-label">Module 5: ML Fundamentals</span><ul class="sidebar-nav"><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module5-lesson1"><span class="sidebar-num">5.1</span> What is ML?</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module5-lesson2"><span class="sidebar-num">5.2</span> Linear Regression</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module5-lesson3"><span class="sidebar-num">5.3</span> Logistic Regression</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module5-lesson4"><span class="sidebar-num">5.4</span> Decision Trees</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module5-lesson5"><span class="sidebar-num">5.5</span> Clustering</a></li></ul><span class="sidebar-section-label">Module 6: Model Training</span><ul class="sidebar-nav"><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module6-lesson1"><span class="sidebar-num">6.1</span> Train/Test Splits</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module6-lesson2"><span class="sidebar-num">6.2</span> Classification Metrics</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module6-lesson3"><span class="sidebar-num">6.3</span> Regression Metrics</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module6-lesson4"><span class="sidebar-num">6.4</span> Hyperparameter Tuning</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module6-lesson5"><span class="sidebar-num">6.5</span> Preventing Overfitting</a></li></ul><span class="sidebar-section-label">Module 7: Deep Learning</span><ul class="sidebar-nav"><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module7-lesson1"><span class="sidebar-num">7.1</span> Neural Networks</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module7-lesson2"><span class="sidebar-num">7.2</span> TensorFlow/Keras</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module7-lesson3"><span class="sidebar-num">7.3</span> CNNs</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module7-lesson4"><span class="sidebar-num">7.4</span> RNNs &amp; LSTMs</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module7-lesson5"><span class="sidebar-num">7.5</span> Transfer Learning</a></li></ul><span class="sidebar-section-label">Module 8: Prompt Engineering</span><ul class="sidebar-nav"><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module8-lesson1"><span class="sidebar-num">8.1</span> Intro to Prompting</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module8-lesson2"><span class="sidebar-num">8.2</span> Zero/Few-Shot &amp; CoT</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module8-lesson3"><span class="sidebar-num">8.3</span> System Prompts</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module8-lesson4"><span class="sidebar-num">8.4</span> Output Formatting</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module8-lesson5"><span class="sidebar-num">8.5</span> Advanced Patterns</a></li></ul><span class="sidebar-section-label">Module 9: LLM Fundamentals</span><ul class="sidebar-nav"><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module9-lesson1"><span class="sidebar-num">9.1</span> How LLMs Work</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module9-lesson2"><span class="sidebar-num">9.2</span> Major LLM Families</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module9-lesson3"><span class="sidebar-num">9.3</span> Tokens &amp; Context</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module9-lesson4"><span class="sidebar-num">9.4</span> Fine-Tuning vs ICL</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module9-lesson5"><span class="sidebar-num">9.5</span> Safety &amp; Limitations</a></li></ul><span class="sidebar-section-label">Module 10: RAG Pipelines</span><ul class="sidebar-nav"><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson1"><span class="sidebar-num">10.1</span> What is RAG?</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson2"><span class="sidebar-num">10.2</span> Document Chunking</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson3"><span class="sidebar-num">10.3</span> Embeddings &amp; Vectors</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson4"><span class="sidebar-num">10.4</span> Building RAG</a></li><li><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module10-lesson5"><span class="sidebar-num">10.5</span> RAG Evaluation</a></li></ul></aside><button class="sidebar-toggle" aria-label="Toggle sidebar">☰</button><main class="main"><div class="container"><section class="hero"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="index.html">Associate</a><span class="sep">›</span><a href="#">Module 3</a><span class="sep">›</span><span>Merging &amp; Joining</span></div><span class="hero-eyebrow">Module 3 · Lesson 7</span><h1>Merging and Joining Datasets</h1><p class="hero-sub">Master combining multiple DataFrames using merge(), join(), and concat(). Learn the different join types (inner, outer, left, right), how to handle key columns, and strategies for concatenating along different axes. These operations are essential when working with data from multiple sources.</p></section><div class="objectives"><h4>Learning Objectives</h4><ul><li>Understand the different join types and when to use each</li><li>Use merge() for flexible SQL-like joins on key columns</li><li>Apply join() for index-based operations</li><li>Concatenate DataFrames along rows and columns with concat()</li><li>Handle mismatched keys and duplicate indices</li><li>Optimize join performance on large datasets</li></ul></div><section class="section" id="merge-basics"><span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span><h2>The merge() Method</h2><p>merge() performs SQL-like joins on key columns. It's the most flexible method for combining DataFrames based on matching values. Think of it like matching data from two sources by a common identifier—customer ID, product code, date, etc.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">pandas</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="im">pd</span>

<span class="cm"># Create sample DataFrames</span>
<span class="va">customers</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({
    <span class="st">'customer_id'</span>: [<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="nm">4</span>],
    <span class="st">'name'</span>: [<span class="st">'Alice'</span>, <span class="st">'Bob'</span>, <span class="st">'Charlie'</span>, <span class="st">'Diana'</span>]
})

<span class="va">orders</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({
    <span class="st">'customer_id'</span>: [<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">5</span>],
    <span class="st">'amount'</span>: [<span class="nm">100</span>, <span class="nm">200</span>, <span class="nm">150</span>, <span class="nm">300</span>]
})

<span class="cm"># Inner join: matching ids only</span>
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">customers</span>.<span class="fn">merge</span>(<span class="va">orders</span>, <span class="va">on</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'customer_id'</span>, <span class="va">how</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'inner'</span>)

<span class="cm"># Left join: keep all customers</span>
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">customers</span>.<span class="fn">merge</span>(<span class="va">orders</span>, <span class="va">on</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'customer_id'</span>, <span class="va">how</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'left'</span>)</code></pre><div class="compare-grid"><div class="compare-item"><div class="compare-title">INNER</div><div class="compare-content">Matching keys only. Most restrictive.</div></div><div class="compare-item"><div class="compare-title">LEFT</div><div class="compare-content">All left rows with matching right rows.</div></div><div class="compare-item"><div class="compare-title">RIGHT</div><div class="compare-content">All right rows with matching left rows.</div></div><div class="compare-item"><div class="compare-title">OUTER</div><div class="compare-content">All rows from both. Most inclusive.</div></div></div><div class="key-concept"><strong>Key Concept:</strong> Use 'on' when key columns have the same name. Use 'left_on' and 'right_on' when names differ.</div></section><section class="section" id="join-method"><span class="section-label">Index-Based Joins</span><h2>The join() Method</h2><p>join() combines DataFrames based on their indices, not column values. This is simpler when indices contain meaningful labels.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="cm"># DataFrames with meaningful indices</span>
<span class="va">prices</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({<span class="st">'price'</span>: [<span class="nm">99</span>, <span class="nm">199</span>]}, <span class="va">index</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">'Widget'</span>, <span class="st">'Gadget'</span>])
<span class="va">stock</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({<span class="st">'qty'</span>: [<span class="nm">50</span>, <span class="nm">30</span>]}, <span class="va">index</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">'Widget'</span>, <span class="st">'Gadget'</span>])

<span class="cm"># Join on index</span>
<span class="va">combined</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">prices</span>.<span class="fn">join</span>(<span class="va">stock</span>, <span class="va">how</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'inner'</span>)</code></pre></section><section class="section" id="concat"><span class="section-label">Concatenation</span><h2>Concatenating with concat()</h2><p>concat() stacks DataFrames vertically (axis=0) or horizontally (axis=1). Unlike merge/join, it doesn't match on keys—it simply aligns them.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="va">jan</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({<span class="st">'sales'</span>: [<span class="nm">100</span>, <span class="nm">150</span>]})
<span class="va">feb</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({<span class="st">'sales'</span>: [<span class="nm">120</span>, <span class="nm">180</span>]})

<span class="cm"># Stack vertically (rows)</span>
<span class="va">all_months</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">concat</span>([<span class="va">jan</span>, <span class="va">feb</span>], <span class="va">axis</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="va">ignore_index</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">True</span>)

<span class="cm"># Stack horizontally (columns)</span>
<span class="va">side_by_side</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">concat</span>([<span class="va">jan</span>, <span class="va">feb</span>], <span class="va">axis</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1</span>)</code></pre><div class="term-grid"><div class="term-item"><div class="term-name">axis=0 (default)</div><div class="term-desc">Stack rows. Use for appending data vertically.</div></div><div class="term-item"><div class="term-name">axis=1</div><div class="term-desc">Stack columns. Use for adding features side-by-side.</div></div><div class="term-item"><div class="term-name">ignore_index=True</div><div class="term-desc">Reset index after concat. Useful when original indices don't matter.</div></div></div></section><section class="section" id="practice"><span class="section-label">Hands-On Practice</span><h2>Complete Data Pipeline</h2><p>Combine customers, orders, and products into one enriched dataset.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="va">customers</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({<span class="st">'cust_id'</span>: [<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>], <span class="st">'name'</span>: [<span class="st">'Alice'</span>, <span class="st">'Bob'</span>]})
<span class="va">orders</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({<span class="st">'cust_id'</span>: [<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>], <span class="st">'prod_id'</span>: [<span class="st">'A'</span>, <span class="st">'B'</span>], <span class="st">'amount'</span>: [<span class="nm">100</span>, <span class="nm">200</span>]})
<span class="va">products</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({<span class="st">'prod_id'</span>: [<span class="st">'A'</span>, <span class="st">'B'</span>], <span class="st">'category'</span>: [<span class="st">'Hardware'</span>, <span class="st">'Software'</span>]})

<span class="va">enriched</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">orders</span>.<span class="fn">merge</span>(<span class="va">customers</span>, <span class="va">on</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'cust_id'</span>).<span class="fn">merge</span>(<span class="va">products</span>, <span class="va">on</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'prod_id'</span>)</code></pre><div class="callout-box tip"><strong>Best Practice:</strong> Always validate row counts before and after joining to catch unexpected results.</div><h3>Exercises</h3><ol><li>Inner join customers and orders. How many rows result?</li><li>Left join to keep all customers, even without orders.</li><li>Concatenate two monthly datasets vertically with reset index.</li><li>Join products with orders then aggregate by category.</li></ol></section><section class="section" id="advanced"><span class="section-label">Advanced Techniques</span><h2>Multi-Key Joins and Complex Merges</h2><p>For complex datasets, you often need to join on multiple columns. Financial data might join on date AND ticker, transaction data on customer AND product AND timestamp. Multiple keys ensure you're matching the right records.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="cm"># Multi-key merge: join on multiple columns</span>
<span class="va">transactions</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({
    <span class="st">'customer_id'</span>: [<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>],
    <span class="st">'date'</span>: [<span class="st">'2024-01-01'</span>, <span class="st">'2024-01-02'</span>, <span class="st">'2024-01-01'</span>, <span class="st">'2024-01-03'</span>],
    <span class="st">'amount'</span>: [<span class="nm">100</span>, <span class="nm">200</span>, <span class="nm">150</span>, <span class="nm">300</span>]
})

<span class="va">daily_limits</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({
    <span class="st">'customer_id'</span>: [<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>],
    <span class="st">'date'</span>: [<span class="st">'2024-01-01'</span>, <span class="st">'2024-01-01'</span>],
    <span class="st">'daily_limit'</span>: [<span class="nm">1000</span>, <span class="nm">500</span>]
})

<span class="cm"># Merge on multiple columns (customer_id AND date)</span>
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">transactions</span>.<span class="fn">merge</span>(<span class="va">daily_limits</span>,
    <span class="va">on</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">'customer_id'</span>, <span class="st">'date'</span>],
    <span class="va">how</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'left'</span>
)</code></pre><section class="section" id="duplicate-handling"><span class="section-label">Data Quality</span><h2>Handling Duplicates and Mismatches</h2><p>Real-world data often has duplicates and mismatches. A customer might have multiple addresses. A transaction might have no corresponding product. Understanding how merge handles these cases is critical for data integrity.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="cm"># Handling duplicate keys</span>
<span class="va">left_data</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({
    <span class="st">'key'</span>: [<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>],  <span class="cm"># key=1 appears twice</span>
    <span class="st">'val_left'</span>: [<span class="st">'A'</span>, <span class="st">'B'</span>, <span class="st">'C'</span>]
})

<span class="va">right_data</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="im">pd</span>.<span class="fn">DataFrame</span>({
    <span class="st">'key'</span>: [<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>],  <span class="cm"># key=2 appears twice</span>
    <span class="st">'val_right'</span>: [<span class="st">'X'</span>, <span class="st">'Y'</span>, <span class="st">'Z'</span>]
})

<span class="cm"># Merge creates cartesian product for duplicate keys</span>
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">left_data</span>.<span class="fn">merge</span>(<span class="va">right_data</span>, <span class="va">on</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'key'</span>)
<span class="cm"># For key=1: 2 left rows × 1 right row = 2 result rows</span>
<span class="cm"># For key=2: 1 left row × 2 right rows = 2 result rows</span>
<span class="cm"># Total: 4 rows (not 3)</span>

<span class="cm"># Check for unexpected duplicates before merging</span>
<span class="va">duplicates</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">left_data</span>[<span class="va">left_data</span>.<span class="fn">duplicated</span>(<span class="st">'key'</span>, <span class="va">keep</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">False</span>)]
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Duplicate keys in left: {len(duplicates)}"</span>)</code></pre><section class="section" id="performance"><span class="section-label">Optimization</span><h2>Join Performance on Large Datasets</h2><p>For millions of rows, join performance matters. Use the right join type, ensure join columns are indexed (sorted), and avoid unnecessary columns. For very large joins, consider databases instead of pandas.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="cm"># Performance tips for large merges</span>

<span class="cm"># 1. Use appropriate join type</span>
<span class="cm"># Inner join faster than left join (less data to process)</span>

<span class="cm"># 2. Pre-filter data before joining</span>
<span class="va">large_left</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df_left</span>[<span class="va">df_left</span>[<span class="st">'status'</span>] <span class="op">==</span> <span class="st">'active'</span>]  <span class="cm"># Filter first</span>
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">large_left</span>.<span class="fn">merge</span>(<span class="va">df_right</span>, <span class="va">on</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'key'</span>)  <span class="cm"># Then merge smaller dataset</span>

<span class="cm"># 3. Use suffixes to handle duplicate column names clearly</span>
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df_left</span>.<span class="fn">merge</span>(<span class="va">df_right</span>,
    <span class="va">on</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'key'</span>,
    <span class="va">suffixes</span><span class="op">=</span>(<span class="st">'_left_src'</span>, <span class="st">'_right_src'</span>)  <span class="cm"># Clear naming</span>
)

<span class="cm"># 4. For very large joins, consider database approach</span>
<span class="cm"># SQLite, PostgreSQL handle large joins efficiently</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">sqlite3</span>

<span class="va">conn</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">sqlite3</span>.<span class="fn">connect</span>(<span class="st">":memory:"</span>)
<span class="va">df_left</span>.<span class="fn">to_sql</span>(<span class="st">'left_table'</span>, <span class="va">conn</span>, <span class="va">index</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">False</span>)
<span class="va">df_right</span>.<span class="fn">to_sql</span>(<span class="st">'right_table'</span>, <span class="va">conn</span>, <span class="va">index</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">False</span>)

<span class="va">query</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"""
SELECT * FROM left_table
INNER JOIN right_table ON left_table.key = right_table.key
"""</span>
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">pd</span>.<span class="fn">read_sql</span>(<span class="va">query</span>, <span class="va">conn</span>)</code></pre><div class="callout-box success"><strong>Pro Tip:</strong> For joins on 10M+ rows, use DuckDB or SQLite. They're faster than pandas for large datasets and handle memory more efficiently.</div><section class="section" id="validation"><span class="section-label">Data Quality</span><h2>Merge Validation Patterns</h2><p>Always validate after merging. Check row counts, look for unexpected nulls in join result, verify no data loss. Build these checks into your data pipeline.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="cm"># Merge validation function</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">validate_merge</span>(<span class="va">left</span>, <span class="va">right</span>, <span class="va">result</span>, <span class="va">join_type</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'left'</span>):
    <span class="st">"""Validate merge operation."""</span>
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Left rows: {len(left)}, Right rows: {len(right)}, Result rows: {len(result)}"</span>)

    <span class="cm"># Check for data loss</span>
    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="va">join_type</span> <span class="op">==</span> <span class="st">'inner'</span> <span class="kw">and</span> <span class="fn">len</span>(<span class="va">result</span>) <span class="op">==</span> <span class="nm">0</span>:
        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"WARNING: Inner join produced no rows!"</span>)

    <span class="cm"># Check for unexpected nulls</span>
    <span class="va">unexpected_nulls</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">result</span>.<span class="fn">isnull</span>().<span class="fn">sum</span>()
    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="va">unexpected_nulls</span>.<span class="fn">sum</span>() <span class="op">&gt;</span> <span class="nm">0</span>:
        <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Nulls in result:\n{unexpected_nulls}"</span>)

    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="kw">True</span>

<span class="cm"># Usage</span>
<span class="va">result</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">customers</span>.<span class="fn">merge</span>(<span class="va">orders</span>, <span class="va">on</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'customer_id'</span>)
<span class="fn">validate_merge</span>(<span class="va">customers</span>, <span class="va">orders</span>, <span class="va">result</span>, <span class="st">'left'</span>)</code></pre><section class="section" id="summary"><span class="section-label">Summary</span><h2>Key Takeaways</h2><ul><li><strong>merge():</strong> SQL-like joins on key columns. Most flexible for value-based matching.</li><li><strong>Join Types:</strong> Inner, Left, Right, Outer. Choose based on data requirements.</li><li><strong>join():</strong> Index-based combining. Simpler when indices are meaningful.</li><li><strong>concat():</strong> Stack DataFrames vertically (axis=0) or horizontally (axis=1). No matching required.</li><li><strong>Multiple Keys:</strong> Join on multiple columns for complex matching scenarios.</li><li><strong>Duplicate Handling:</strong> Understand cartesian products when keys appear multiple times.</li><li><strong>Performance:</strong> Filter before joining, use inner joins when possible, consider databases for 10M+ rows.</li><li><strong>Validation:</strong> Check row counts, look for nulls, verify no unexpected data loss.</li></ul></section></div><div class="nav-buttons"><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module3-lesson6" class="nav-btn prev"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M15 18l-6-6 6-6"/></svg> Previous</a><a href="../learn.html?c=associate&l=module4-lesson1" class="nav-btn next">Next <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></div></div></main><footer><div class="container"><div class="footer-logo"><img src="../../assets/logo_only.png" alt="SustainSys" class="footer-logo-img"><div class="footer-logo-text">Sustain<span>Sys</span> AI Academy</div></div><p>© 2026 SustainSys. All rights reserved.</p><p><a href="https://sustainsys.co.uk">sustainsys.co.uk</a></p></div></footer><script src="../assets/course-nav.js"><\/script></body></html>
